<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naftaly Frenkel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Naftaly Aronovich Frenkel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naftaly Frenkel was a Soviet security officer of the secret police, best known for organising labour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. His origins are uncertain; his prisoner card gives his birthplace as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a>. Arrested in 1923 and sentenced to 10 years at the Solovki camp, he rose from prisoner to camp commander and developed the nourishment scale, which divided prisoners by their capacity for work and fed them accordingly, so that in practice it sorted those who would survive from those who would die. He then directed the daily work on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea-baltic-canal">White Sea–Baltic Canal</a> from November 1931, headed the BAMlag railway camp by 1937, and led the railway construction directorate from 1937 to 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naftaly-frenkel.jpg" alt="Naftaly Frenkel" width="200" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naftaly_Aronovich_Frenkel_(1883_–1960).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Accounts of Frenkel's background differ widely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</a> called him a Turkish Jew born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; others made him a Hungarian manufacturer, or a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> or the land of Israel, and the Finnish communist Arvo Tuominen, who met him in 1933, claimed he was related to the Swedish-speaking Finnish family of the Frenckells. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> he described himself as a merchant. His arrest in 1923 for illegally crossing borders covered smuggling, or perhaps being too successful a merchant, and he was sent to the camps of the Solovetsky Islands, known by the acronym SLON.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>How he rose is unclear. One story has him posting a detailed critique of the camp's industries in the complaints box, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a>. By November 1924 the administration was asking for his early release, granted in 1927. Solzhenitsyn credits him with inventing the you-eat-as-you-work system, while many historians treat tales of his power as legend; Anne Applebaum wrote that he found a way to turn a prison camp into an apparently profitable economic institution. Under the scale those fit for heavy work received 800 grams of bread and 80 grams of meat, and invalids half that.</p><p>Frenkel dropped re-education, closed the camp journals and put criminal and political prisoners to work side by side on road building and tree felling. His Economic-Commercial Department outbid a civilian forestry undertaking in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, angering the local authorities, though in 1929 the camp in fact ran a deficit of 1.6 million roubles. Fellow communists accused him of running a personal network of spies, and one commander admitted he had thought of murdering him. From November 1931 he managed the daily work on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea-baltic-canal">White Sea–Baltic Canal</a>, using many prisoners brought from Solovetsky.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>While nearly all his former colleagues died in the purges, Frenkel was protected at the highest level, apparently by Stalin; when 48 Trotskyists were arrested at BAMlag in 1938 his case was delayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. As a protégé of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> he headed railway construction, received the Order of Lenin three times and was discharged for ill health on 28 April 1947. He lived reclusively in Moscow in the 1950s and died in 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naftaly Frenkel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Naftaly Frenkel</b>’s “nourishment scale”, which tied food to output, often killed weaker prisoners within weeks.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Istanbul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5258 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Naftaly Frenkel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
